#
# What we vendor from BRouter, and what we deliberately leave behind.
#
# Read by pull-brouter.sh. Each line names one of BRouter's Gradle modules and
# the single package it publishes; the package tree is copied into
# routing-brouter/src/main/java/ under its ORIGINAL name, so a refresh is a file
# copy and upstream diffs still apply — the same rule the Gadgetbridge vendor
# follows.
#
# Why vendor at all, when JitPack publishes these five as jars: JitPack builds
# them itself from a git tag, which makes them prebuilt binaries of third-party
# provenance. This app targets F-Droid, whose builders compile everything from
# source out of repositories they trust, and the rest of the dependency list is
# held to that same standard (see the comments in gradle/libs.versions.toml).
# 19k lines of MIT-licensed Java that changes a few times a year is a cheaper
# thing to carry than an exception to that rule.
#
# Syntax:
#   include <module> <package-path>
#   (blank lines and # comments ignored)

# The route search itself: RoutingEngine, RoutingContext, the cost models, and
# the formatters. FormatJson is the one that matters most here — it produces the
# same GeoJSON shape the server's BRouter returns, so an offline route and an
# online one are the same document to everything downstream.
include brouter-core btools/router

# Reading rd5 files: the tile grid, the node cache, waypoint matching.
include brouter-mapaccess btools/mapaccess

# The .brf profile language — parsing it and evaluating it per way.
include brouter-expressions btools/expressions

# Shared primitives (compact maps, the sorted heap the search runs on, the
# cheap-ruler distance approximations). Small, and everything else needs it.
include brouter-codec btools/codec
include brouter-util btools/util

# NOT vendored:
#
#   brouter-server        The HTTP front end. Tracks already runs it as a
#                         container for the browser and for phones with signal;
#                         on the phone we call the engine directly.
#   brouter-routing-app   BRouter's own Android app and its Android service
#                         interface. We are the app.
#   brouter-map-creator   Builds rd5 from OSM PBF. Enormous, and the server
#                         downloads finished segments from brouter.de instead.
#   misc/profiles2        The .brf profiles and lookups.dat. These are *data*
#                         that must match the rd5 files, so the phone fetches
#                         them from the Tracks server (which fetches the same
#                         files the brouter container uses) rather than pinning
#                         a copy at vendoring time.
